<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chincha Islands</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chincha Islands are a group of three small islands off the southern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, near Pisco, whose vast deposits of seabird guano made them Peru's chief source of wealth from the 1840s. Guano dug there, largely by Chinese indentured labourers, was exported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> as fertiliser. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s seizure of the islands in 1864 led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chincha-islands-war">Chincha Islands War</a>, and by the 1870s the deposits were nearly exhausted.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chincha-islands.jpg" alt="Chincha Islands" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gabriele Giuseppini · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chincha_Islands_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
